scope: |
  SSC3-14c — `import a.b.{X, Y}`, the owner's decision of 2026-08-14: "списки имен в импортах
  должны работать". Today both fronts refuse it inside a fence and only v3's refuses it in a bare
  file. (`v3/SPRINT.md` is still claimed by `bench-b4-three-version-table`, blocked on host load, so
  the plan is here — in git, before the edit — as it was for SSC3-14b.)

  IT WILL NOT MOVE N, AND THAT IS MEASURED BEFORE THE EDIT RATHER THAN DISCOVERED AFTER IT. This is
  the varargs lesson applied one task later. The only selector-list imports the corpus can reach are
  three lines in `std/mapreduce/{distributed,typed,shuffle}.ssc`, and all three name
  `scalascript.typeddata`, which is a JVM BACKEND SCALA PACKAGE
  (`backend/typed-data/src/main/scala/scalascript/typeddata`) and not an `.ssc` module. Probed with
  the SUPPORTED spelling, which produces the identical target:

      import scalascript.typeddata.*
      -> cannot find the import 'std/scalascript/typeddata.ssc' — looked in: …

  So accepting the selector list exchanges one honest refusal for another honest refusal. A refusal
  short-circuits; what it hides is another refusal. Expect PASS/DIFF/CRASH unchanged.

  WHAT IT DOES BUY, and this is the reason to do it beyond the owner's say-so: a real I-3
  divergence. In a BARE `.ssc` — no fence, so `Loader.imports` never scans it as code and its
  `inCode` guard never fires — `import std.geo.{Point, Region}` is REFUSED by v3's own front at
  `Parser.scala:1822` and SILENTLY DROPPED by uniml, which then runs and prints. One language, two
  answers, chosen by whether `v3/.jars/uniml.cp` exists. The control says the divergence is specific
  to this spelling and not to bare files: with the supported `import std.geo.*` both fronts print
  `ok`.

  ONE EDIT, because there are three sites and they already share a decider. `Source.scalaImportPath`
  is what `Loader` consults (its refusal, `Loader.scala:60`), what `Source.blankIfImport` consults,
  and therefore what decides whether the leftover ever reaches `Parser.scala:1822` at all — that
  refusal only ever sees spellings `blankIfImport` did not blank. Widening the one function closes
  all three without a second decision site.

  NORMALISE THE LIST TO `.*` rather than carrying it. The refusal's own text says why — "an import
  brings the WHOLE module either way" — so `a.b.{X, Y}` becomes the path `a.b.*`, which makes
  `Loader.scalaImportTarget` derive the module by the code path that already exists instead of a new
  one that happens to agree.

  IN SCOPE: single-line lists, with and without an `as` rename INSIDE the list —
  `std/mapreduce/shuffle.ssc:46` writes `{DatasetWire, DatasetWirePartition, JsonValue as
  TJsonValue}`. OUT OF SCOPE and stated rather than silently unhandled: a MULTI-LINE list
  (`import x.{` then names on following lines) needs cross-line state in a line-at-a-time scanner;
  the only ones in the tree are in `examples/`, which the corpus does not load, so nothing that runs
  needs it. Also out of scope: `import a.b as c` — a rename WITHOUT a list — because the owner asked
  for lists and the narrow-claim rule says take what was asked.

  MEASUREMENT: N with floors DIFF 3 and CRASH 9 against the same tree minus exactly this change, and
  the bare-file divergence probed on BOTH fronts before and after. Gates green is not evidence about
  N — v3's gates do not cover the corpus.
  WIDENED 2026-08-14 to `v3/src/Loader.scala` and `v3/src/Parser.scala`, BEFORE the edit, for the
  DIAGNOSTIC and nothing else. Both carry a copy of the refusal text, and it now says selector lists
  "have no meaning here" — false the moment this lands. `Parser.scala:1814` warns about exactly this
  in its own comment: a stale message is the dangerous kind, because a reader deletes a line that
  works. The decision logic stays in `Source.scalaImportPath`; these two edits are the wording.
  WIDENED 2026-08-14 to `v3/tests/front`, BEFORE the edit, because `front-gate` went RED and it is
  RIGHT to: `v3/tests/front/scala-import-selector.ssc` is a NEGATIVE fixture asserting the old
  decision, and its prose spells that decision out ("A selector list has no meaning here —
  REFUSED"). The gate needs no script change — its rule is that a fixture with no `.expected` must
  be refused — so flipping it means rewriting the fixture's prose and giving it an `.expected`.
  Found by running the gates, not by reading the diff; it is the one gate that encodes this
  decision.
next: flip the negative fixture, re-run front-gate, then push
